you're watching the daily climate show  on  sky news on today's programme families  could be given  cash to help offset their energy bills  under plans being considered by the  government  the sinking brothel of bangladesh we  report from banishante  where climate change has pushed many  women into prostitution  and how your pension could help save the  [Music]  planet  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those developing the solutions  now the government is considering  paying families to help them offset the  rising cost of gas bills  and encourage a switch to green energy  now around  19 million homes in the uk need to be  made  more efficient in order for the  government to hit its 2050 net  zero target now under the proposed  scheme low and middle income  families will be paid a set amount to  compensate  for increases in gas bills that will  come from the drive to cut emissions  it would also be funded by a new carbon  tax  and all families would be paid the same  regardless of their individual emissions  it means households who switched to  cheaper green energy  would still get the full payment meaning  that they could pocket the difference  well here to explain more is our climate  correspondent hannah thomas peter  so hannah tell us more about how this  scheme might work  well this is as you said part of a  government strategy to  address a really big logistical problem  which is that we've got 19 million homes  in this country that need to essentially  be given  a green retrofit in the coming years if  the government is to have  any chance at all of hitting its net  zero emissions by 2050  target the one that we hear so much  about now  right now there's a really complicated  set of schemes and potential grants that  you can apply for if you want to get a  heat pump for example  or if you want to put special insulation  in your homes but there's not one  overarching homes and heating policy  just listen to one person we've spoken  to about the challenges  of green retrofits when you're sort of  doing it on your own without government  help  i would guess that the the renovations  so far are in the 10  000 to 20 000 pound range  i've done a lot of the work myself and  with the help of  friends and family so i imagine that it  could be  a bit more than that i would say that  this is a fairly small house  and there are much larger homes that  require a much deeper  retrofit so the cost can rise  so boris johnson knows that he has got  to find a way to incentivize green  behavior  and it's not just a practical problem  it's a political one as well this scheme  that the government  is considering is about making sure that  all those  new conservative voters in particular  low-income homeowners in particular  aren't suddenly faced with unreasonably  high costs  as this country races to go green  hannah thank you and you can find out  more about how the scheme would work on  our website just head to skynews.com  climate let's take a look now at the  day's other climate news and the  australian government says there are  grounds to appeal a judgment  declaring young people must be protected  from pollutants  from a coal mine expansion if approved  the judge said the country's environment  minister susan lay  must take reasonable care to avoid  causing personal injury or death  to those under 18.  the uk is ranked sixth in the world for  the share of electricity generated by  wind and solar  these renewables accounted for 29 of  britain's electricity production last  year according to analysis by the think  tank ember  electricity generated by wind and solar  has doubled over the last five years and  now provides almost a tenth of the  world's electricity  the co-op has introduced in-store  recycling across the uk  in a bid to reduce plastic waste the  launch of the recycling points  follows a successful trial in 50 stores  that saw  86 percent of customers use the service  crisp packets and yogurt lids are  amongst the items that  can be collected through the scheme  which also accepts food packaging from  other retailers  the university of bristol has become the  first university in the world to achieve  institutional green labs certification  it means the university's laboratories  are reducing the impact on the climate  through using  more efficient equipment and better  waste management  although labs are essential for climate  research they  have five times more environmental  impact  compared to office spaces  and the london symphonetta orchestra are  staging a concert  totally off-grid instead the performance  called houseslide will  be powered entirely by bicycles 16 bikes  will  be used to generate energy for the  performance which explores one woman's  journey  tackling the climate crisis cyclists  will need to pedal non-stop for 40  minutes during the show  which is held at london's royal festival  hall  bangladesh is one of the world's most  densely populated countries and is  hugely vulnerable to climate change  over a quarter of the population lives  on the coast that's around 45  million people and flooding caused by  rising sea levels and made worse by  cyclones  is a huge problem last year alone  4.4 million internal displacements in  the country  were caused by disasters now that number  accounts for people sometimes displaced  more than once  due to multiple extreme weather events  now we heard last week from the capital  dhaka where families are forced into  living in slums far from their  families and communities others end up  in even more difficult circumstances  some women on the run from rising sea  levels have been pushed into a life of  prostitution  and now near the village of bani shanta  they're in danger of being trapped by  the water again  from where skies katherine vitotse  reports  the name of this place is bani shanta it  means  a message of calm but on this narrow  strip of land there is little peace  these huts form one of the country's  oldest brothels  here women call themselves girls  many have been trafficked some because  of the impact of climate change  when pervin was 15 her family's coastal  home and farm were destroyed through  rising sea levels  the loss made the family desperate which  made pervin a target  for sex traffickers  legacy  around a hundred women work in bani  shanta  and although brothels are legal in  bangladesh they are still taboo  sex workers and their children are  hugely stigmatized  here at least there is a sense of  community  but climate change is eroding that too  their land is disappearing chipped away  by rising sea levels  and hit by a vicious cyclone last year  the chief madam here tells us they can't  move further back  they're stuck between a river that  floods them to the front  and behind a village that doesn't want  them and where they couldn't work  climate change affects us all but it  also  exposes vulnerabilities and affects some  so much more than others katarina  vertotsi sky news in banishchanter  bangladesh  and you can see more from bangladesh on  our weekly digital show we go behind the  scenes of katarina's trip to a country  on the front line  of the climate crisis that's available  on sky news social channels our app  and website now from going vegan to  reducing the amount of flights you take  many of us are making small changes to  the way we live our lives  to help save the planet but what about  choosing to go  green with your pension moving your  investments to sustainable funds  could have a far greater impact on the  environment according to the campaign  group  make my money matter now they estimate  that it could save  19 tons of carbon per year more than  double the average uk  carbon footprint well joining me now is  richard curtis  film director and co-founder of make my  money matter  welcome to you richard curtis you've  you're known of course for your film  directing so what's got you interested  in green pensions  oh look you know the other thing i've  done is um  i started red nose day and i'm a un  advocate for the sustainable goals and  i'm as passionate as most people you  know suddenly are about climate  and everything that we can actually do  about it rather than just hoping that  our  politicians take action faster and so i  got really interested in the issue  of financial footprint having an effect  on your  carbon footprint well yes and it's  perhaps not the most obvious weapon  against  climate change but what difference do  you think that having a green pension  can make  well it's incredible actually because  all the money that we've got in pensions  is invested i don't think when i started  looking into this i  knew even that which means that you have  a choice to invest in  all the companies all the things that  you can make a difference you know so  your money won't be contributing to  deforestation it'll be contributing to  renewable energy you know you'll be in a  affordable housing you'll be supporting  all sorts of things that can really help  the planet without doing anything apart  from making the initial  demand or choice to change your pension  into a sustainable one so  it's literally the biggest weapon that  most individuals have  to personally have an effect on climate  change  is it a complicated process though  switching your pension to  being sustainable how do you know  whether or not it is  well uh i mean your pension provider  knows  and uh it can be complicated it can be  easy my  instinct is that it's easy for people  who have financial advisors to say this  is what i want  most people have their pensions through  their companies and companies are  increasingly seeing that as part of  their  sustainable responsibilities so  the thing is to ask and with any luck  now  increasing number of pension providers  will say oh yeah well look if that's  what you want we won't give you a  default pension that may be in  arms and gambling and deforestation and  fossil fuels will give one which is in  renewable housing renewable energy  um you know sustainable food and all  those things so  it's it's part of a process that is  gradually becoming easier  well richard curtis really interesting  to talk to you thanks very much indeed  are you really welcome good to talk to  you that's everything from us for this  week but don't forget to check out  climatecast over the weekend  and in the latest episode find out what  climate change and donuts  have got in common you can find that  wherever you normally get your podcast  from  thanks for watching and see you next  [Music]  week

